---
// The one rule, in its own component because it is stated at more than one
// altitude on this page and must not drift between them: the summary here, the
// standing note on S7, and the required check V5 all say the same thing.
//
// It is a component rather than prose in the section for a plainer reason too —
// "replace all records" is the single action in this whole migration that would
// do immediate, silent damage, and a distinct file is harder to edit past
// without noticing than a paragraph in the middle of a longer section.
---

<div class="rule">
  <div class="k">The one rule</div>
  <h3>Email must not break.</h3>
  <p>
    <code>@harvardintech.com</code> email runs on HostGator, separately from the
    website, and it is configured through the same registrar screen we'll be
    editing. The one action that would do real damage is the tempting one:
    <b>"replace all records."</b> That would take <code>nadia@harvardintech.com</code>
    and every other mailbox offline, and mail sent during the outage may simply
    bounce rather than queue.
  </p>
  <p>
    Only two things change: the <b>apex <code>A</code> records</b> and the
    <b><code>www</code> <code>CNAME</code></b>. The <code>MX</code> record, the
    <code>mail.</code> host record, and the SPF <code>TXT</code> record are never
    touched. Step <b>S7</b> spells out exactly which lines to edit, and
    <b>V5</b> makes proving mail still flows a required step before the cutover
    is called done — not a thing we check afterwards if someone complains.
  </p>
</div>

